    pr_ref:  # 282c
    description: "WHAT. PR #2200 made each analyzer hand the whole type text to the shared normalizers in core analyze/base.py. Three shared helpers still reduce distinct types to one text:\n- strip_fqn_prefix keeps the last dotted segment: Java `Foo.Bar` = `Baz.Bar`; Swift `Int.Type` = `Type`, and a dotted name inside a tuple or function type; C# a nullable or array tuple with a qualified element, and a `ref` return; Kotlin function types, `suspend` types, annotated types and dotted receivers; Scala six pairs listed in the Scala package report; Groovy annotated types. It also swallows the parameters of a function type.\n- split_params_top_level treats `<` as a bracket and ignores braces (Go struct and interface literals; a Dart default value containing a comma leaves a fragment in the hashed text, and `{int a: 1}` hashes `a:`).\n- _find_matching_paren does not skip string literals.\n- skip_self: Rust `self` = `&mut self`.\n\nSEEN. Reported by the Java, Kotlin, Swift, C#, Scala, Go, Groovy and Dart packages on fixtures; not sized on real repositories.\n\nNOT COVERED by the owner's 2026-11-16 ruling (which named the nine analyzers' own renderers), so left filed. Fixing a part marked MOVES IDS changes hashed input, so it belongs to the next stable-id bump, with a measured churn figure in the spec's history row.\n\nFound during PR 2 of the stable-id plan (the v15 bump), 2026-11-16 (PR #2200). Detail: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/stable_id_items_review_11152026/pr2/STATUS.md."  # 282c
    fields: {}  # 282c
